  "worked_solution": "Since $x>5,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >5 \\\\ x-3 & >5-3 \\\\ x-3 & >2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $x-3$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>5).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $x-3,$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{x-3} & \\le 2 \\\\ (x-3)\\cdot \\frac{x}{x-3} & \\le (x-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\ x & \\le 2x-6 \\\\ -x & \\le -6 \\\\ x & \\ge 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>5$ and $x\\ge 6.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x\\ge 6.$",
